1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
If you took off your helmet in space, you wouldn't explode. What actually happens to your body is so much worse… and it starts in about ten seconds.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
First, the air rips out of your lungs. Hold your breath, and they tear apart. The water on your tongue and eyes instantly starts to boil — not from heat, but from the total lack of pressure.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Your skin puffs up to nearly twice its size as the water under it turns to vapor. You don't pop. You just… swell. And the whole time, you're completely silent, because sound can't travel out here.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
You'd stay conscious for about fifteen seconds — just long enough to watch it happen. Then your blood, starved of oxygen, quietly shuts your brain off. And space doesn't even notice.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Because out here, you are unimaginably small. This is Earth. Everything that has ever happened to anyone, ever, happened on this one quiet rock.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Now watch it shrink. The Moon looks close — but you could fit every single planet in our solar system in the gap between the Earth and the Moon, with room to spare.

7
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:26,000
And the thing holding all of it together is the Sun. It's so large that one million Earths would fit inside it. To the Sun, our planet is a grain of sand.

8
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Light is the fastest thing that exists. It could circle the Earth seven times in a single second. And yet, sunlight still takes over eight minutes to reach you.

9
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So when you look at the Sun, you're seeing it as it was eight minutes ago. If it suddenly vanished, you'd have no idea — for eight more minutes.

10
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Now leave the solar system entirely. The nearest star to ours is so far away that its light takes four years to get here — traveling at the fastest speed in the universe.

11
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Going there with our fastest spacecraft would take about seventy thousand years. Nobody who left would ever arrive. Their great-great-grandchildren wouldn't either.

12
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And that's just one star. Our galaxy holds around four hundred billion of them — spread across a disk so wide that light takes a hundred thousand years just to cross it.

13
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Zoom out again. Our entire galaxy is just one dot. There are an estimated two trillion galaxies in the observable universe — each one full of billions of stars.

14
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:54,000
There are more stars in the sky than there are grains of sand on every beach on Earth. And around many of them are worlds we will never, ever see.

15
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Now, the scary part. Scattered through all of this are black holes — places where gravity got so strong that not even light is fast enough to escape.

16
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:19,000
If you fell into one, gravity would pull your feet harder than your head. You'd be stretched into a thin strand of atoms. Scientists actually call this… spaghettification.

17
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:32,000
And our own galaxy has a supermassive one at its center — four million times heavier than the Sun — with everything, including us, slowly orbiting around it.

18
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Meanwhile, our entire galaxy is being dragged through space toward something we can't even see. They named it The Great Attractor. We still don't know what it is.

19
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:58,000
And the universe is stretching. Every distant galaxy is racing away from us, faster and faster. Most are already moving away faster than their light can ever reach us.

20
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Which means there are galaxies we can see tonight that we are, right now, watching disappear forever. We will never know what happened to them.

21
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Far enough into the future, the sky will go dark. Every other galaxy will have vanished beyond reach, and anyone alive then will think the Milky Way is the entire universe.

22
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Eventually, even the stars burn out. The universe drifts toward total cold and silence — a darkness with no light, no heat, and no one left to notice.

23
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:50,000
And here's the part that should keep you up tonight: everything I just showed you — every star, every galaxy — is only about five percent of what's out there.

24
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:03,000
The other ninety-five percent is invisible. We can't see it, we can't touch it, and we have absolutely no idea what it is. Sleep well.
